$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The conviction below</h2>
<p>The prosecution&rsquo;s case was that on 20 November 2020 the appellant took the victim to a secluded place and committed penetrative sexual assault. The Sessions Judge (Principal POCSO Court), Salem convicted him on 30 December 2025: twenty years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment under Section 5(m) read with Section 6 of the POCSO Act, five years under Section 366 IPC for kidnapping, and a month under Section 342 IPC for wrongful confinement, sentences to run concurrently. He went into custody the day of the judgment.</p>

<h2>What the High Court saw in the record</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The victim&rsquo;s own words.</strong> Both her statement before the Magistrate and her deposition at trial said the appellant hugged and kissed her. Neither described a penetrative act — the ingredient on which the Section 6 conviction and the twenty-year term rest. The defence had argued the facts, at their highest, fit Section 9(m) r/w 10 (aggravated sexual assault), a materially lighter offence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Unexplained delay.</strong> The complaint was lodged on 27 November 2020, about seven days after the alleged incident, with no explanation on record.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A distant appeal.</strong> The appellant had been inside since 30 December 2025, and the appeal was unlikely to be taken up soon. Continued incarceration pending a doubtful conviction weighed in favour of suspension.</div>
</div>

<h2>Suspension, not acquittal</h2>
<p>Section 430(1) of the BNSS empowers the appellate court to suspend execution of a sentence and release the appellant on bail pending appeal. The jurisprudence asks for more than arguable grounds in grave offences &mdash; something approaching a patent infirmity. A conviction for penetrative assault resting on testimony that describes none is that kind of infirmity, at least prima facie. The court was careful to decide only the interim question; the appeal itself will be heard on the full record, where the prosecution may yet defend the trial court&rsquo;s reading.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Match the sentence to the deposition.</strong> In POCSO appeals, the first exercise is laying the charge, the conviction and the victim&rsquo;s actual testimony side by side. A mismatch between the offence found and the acts described is the strongest suspension ground there is.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Delay still matters.</strong> Delay in complaint is never fatal by itself, but unexplained delay remains a legitimate factor at the suspension stage, where probabilities are weighed, not proved.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody arithmetic counts.</strong> Where the appeal will realistically wait years, courts increasingly refuse to let the sentence run ahead of appellate scrutiny in doubtful cases.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This report summarises an interim order of the Madras High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. The appeal remains pending, and nothing here comments on its eventual outcome.</div>
